IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ip-nouns`
IMPORT `ip-framework`
IMPORT `ip-rule-of-reason`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for the Licensing of Intellectual Property (2017), parts 5 and 6:
-- particular restraints, the acquisition of rights, and invalid rights.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. 5.0 [pdf page 28]: the restraints listed "are not
-- intended as an exhaustive list of practices that could raise competition concerns".
-- Each result keeps the modal of its sentence; where the text states no outcome, the
-- answer is NOTHING.

§ `Part 5: application of the general principles; part 6: invalid rights`

§§ `5.1 Horizontal restraints`

-- 5.1 [pdf page 28]: "Following the general principles outlined in section 3.4, the
-- Agencies will often evaluate horizontal restraints under the rule of reason.
-- Additionally, some restraints may merit per se treatment, including price-fixing,
-- allocation of markets or customers, agreements to reduce output, and certain group
-- boycotts." The test is 3.4's; the types are in `Type of restraint`.
@ref 5.1 [pdf page 28]
GIVEN r IS A `Restraint in a licensing arrangement`
GIVETH A `Mode of analysis (3.4)`
`5.1: the mode of analysis of a horizontal restraint` MEANS
    `3.4: the mode of analysis` r

§§ `5.2 Price maintenance`

DECLARE `What section 5.2 says of price maintenance` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis, case by case, evaluating the competitive benefits and harms`
    `the agencies will consider the agreement per se illegal: it constitutes a horizontal cartel`

-- 5.2 [pdf pages 29-30]: "the Agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis to price
-- maintenance in intellectual property licensing agreements ... Agreements constituting
-- a horizontal cartel will be considered per se illegal." Not a condition on the resale
-- price: 5.2 does not apply (NOTHING). State law (n.65) is not encoded.
@ref 5.2 [pdf pages 29-30]
GIVEN p IS A `Price maintenance facts (5.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.2 says of price maintenance`
`5.2: price maintenance` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT p's `the licensor conditions the license on the resale price of the product incorporating the licensed technology` THEN NOTHING
      IF p's `the agreement constitutes a horizontal cartel` THEN JUST `the agencies will consider the agreement per se illegal: it constitutes a horizontal cartel`
      OTHERWISE JUST `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis, case by case, evaluating the competitive benefits and harms`

§§ `5.3 Tying arrangements`

-- 5.3 [pdf pages 30-31]: conditioning a license on the purchase of another item of
-- intellectual property, a good or a service; a package license is a tie "if the
-- licensing of one intellectual property right is conditioned upon the acceptance of a
-- license of another, separate intellectual property right", and is then evaluated
-- "under the same principles".
@ref 5.3 [pdf pages 30-31]
GIVEN t IS A `Tying facts (5.3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `5.3: the arrangement is a tying arrangement` IF
    t's `the license of one item is conditioned on the licensee's purchase or license of another, separate item`

DECLARE `What section 5.3 says of a tying arrangement` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies would be likely to challenge the tying arrangement`

-- 5.3 [pdf pages 30-31]: "The Agencies would be likely to challenge a tying arrangement if:
-- (1) the seller has market power in the tying product, (2) the arrangement has an
-- adverse effect on competition in the relevant market for the tying product or the tied
-- product, and (3) efficiency justifications for the arrangement do not outweigh the
-- anticompetitive effects. The Agencies will not presume that a patent, copyright, or
-- trade secret necessarily confers market power upon its owner." A sufficient condition
-- only: when one of the three fails, the text states no outcome (NOTHING), and it does not
-- say the agencies will not challenge. Whether the tying item is protected is not read.
@ref 5.3 [pdf pages 30-31]; 2.2 [pdf page 6]
GIVEN t IS A `Tying facts (5.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.3 says of a tying arrangement`
`5.3: tying` MEANS
    IF     `5.3: the arrangement is a tying arrangement` t
       AND t's `the seller has market power in the tying product`
       AND t's `the arrangement has an adverse effect on competition in the relevant market for the tying product or the tied product`
       AND (NOT t's `efficiency justifications for the arrangement outweigh its anticompetitive effects`)
    THEN JUST `the agencies would be likely to challenge the tying arrangement`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `5.4 Exclusive dealing`

DECLARE `What section 5.4 says of exclusive dealing` IS ONE OF
    `exclusive dealing arrangements are evaluated under the rule of reason`

-- 5.4 [pdf page 31]: "In the intellectual property context, exclusive dealing occurs when
-- a license prevents the licensee from licensing, selling, distributing, or using
-- competing technologies. Exclusive dealing arrangements are evaluated under the rule of
-- reason." What is exclusive dealing in effect is 4.1.2's.
@ref 5.4 [pdf page 31]; 4.1.2 [pdf pages 23-24]
GIVEN l IS A `License terms and structure (4.1.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.4 says of exclusive dealing`
`5.4: exclusive dealing` MEANS
    IF `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive dealing` l
    THEN JUST `exclusive dealing arrangements are evaluated under the rule of reason`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Matter the agencies take into account for exclusive dealing (5.4)` IS ONE OF
    `promotion of the exploitation and development of the licensor's technology`
    `anticompetitive foreclosure of, or constraint on competition among, competing technologies`
    `encouragement of licensees to develop and market the licensed technology or its specialized applications`
    `greater incentives for licensors to develop or refine the licensed technology`
    `other increases in competition and output in a relevant market`

-- 5.4 [pdf pages 31-32]: "the Agencies will take into account the extent to which the
-- arrangement (1) promotes the exploitation and development of the licensor's technology
-- and (2) anticompetitively forecloses ..."; and, if it "may have an anticompetitive
-- effect, they will evaluate the extent to which the restraint encourages licensees ...,
-- increases licensors' incentives ..., or otherwise increases competition and enhances
-- output". The market characteristics the likelihood is related to are reported by
-- `4.1.1: the considerations bearing on the risk of harm that are present`. A report
-- only, in the text's order; no weighing.
@ref 5.4 [pdf pages 31-32]
GIVEN e IS A `Exclusive dealing facts (5.4)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Matter the agencies take into account for exclusive dealing (5.4)`
`5.4: the matters present` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF e's `the arrangement promotes the exploitation and development of the licensor's technology` THEN JUST `promotion of the exploitation and development of the licensor's technology` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `the arrangement anticompetitively forecloses the exploitation and development of, or otherwise constrains competition among, competing technologies` THEN JUST `anticompetitive foreclosure of, or constraint on competition among, competing technologies` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `the restraint encourages licensees to develop and market the licensed technology or specialized applications of it` THEN JUST `encouragement of licensees to develop and market the licensed technology or its specialized applications` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `the restraint increases licensors' incentives to develop or refine the licensed technology` THEN JUST `greater incentives for licensors to develop or refine the licensed technology` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF e's `the restraint otherwise increases competition and enhances output in a relevant market` THEN JUST `other increases in competition and output in a relevant market` ELSE NOTHING))

§§ `5.5 Cross-licensing and pooling arrangements`

-- 5.5 [pdf page 32]: "When cross-licensing or pooling arrangements are mechanisms to
-- accomplish naked price-fixing or market division, they are subject to challenge under
-- the per se rule"; collective price or output restraints "may be deemed unlawful if they
-- do not contribute to an efficiency-enhancing integration". The test is 3.4's.
@ref 5.5 [pdf page 32]
GIVEN r IS A `Restraint in a licensing arrangement`
GIVETH A `Mode of analysis (3.4)`
`5.5: the mode of analysis of a collective restraint in a pool or cross-license` MEANS
    `3.4: the mode of analysis` r

DECLARE `What section 5.5 says of a settlement by cross-license` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may challenge the settlement as an unlawful restraint of trade`

-- 5.5 [pdf page 32]: "When such cross-licensing involves horizontal competitors, ... the
-- Agencies will consider whether the effect of the settlement is to diminish competition
-- ... In the absence of offsetting efficiencies, such settlements may be challenged as
-- unlawful restraints of trade." No other case has a stated outcome (NOTHING).
@ref 5.5 [pdf page 32]
GIVEN s IS A `Settlement by cross-license (5.5)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.5 says of a settlement by cross-license`
`5.5: a settlement by cross-license` MEANS
    IF     s's `the settlement involves the cross-licensing of intellectual property rights between horizontal competitors`
       AND s's `the effect of the settlement is to diminish competition among entities that would have been actual or potential competitors in a relevant market in the absence of the cross-license`
       AND (NOT s's `there are offsetting efficiencies`)
    THEN JUST `the agencies may challenge the settlement as an unlawful restraint of trade`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 5.5 says of exclusion from a pool` IS ONE OF
    `exclusion is unlikely to have anticompetitive effects`
    `the agencies will evaluate whether the limitations on participation are reasonably related to the efficient development and exploitation of the pooled technologies, and will assess their net effect in the relevant market`

-- 5.5 [pdf page 33]: "Pooling arrangements generally need not be open to all who would like
-- to join ... In general, exclusion from a pooling or cross-licensing arrangement among
-- competing technologies is unlikely to have anticompetitive effects unless (1) excluded
-- firms cannot effectively compete in the relevant market for the good incorporating the
-- licensed technologies and (2) the pool participants collectively possess market power
-- in the relevant market. If these circumstances exist, the Agencies will evaluate ..."
-- Both sides are stated. A pool that is not among competing technologies is outside the
-- sentence (NOTHING).
@ref 5.5 [pdf page 33]
GIVEN x IS A `Exclusion from a pool or cross-license (5.5)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.5 says of exclusion from a pool`
`5.5: exclusion from a pool or cross-license` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT x's `the arrangement is among competing technologies` THEN NOTHING
      IF     x's `excluded firms cannot effectively compete in the relevant market for the good incorporating the licensed technologies`
         AND x's `the pool participants collectively possess market power in the relevant market`
        THEN JUST `the agencies will evaluate whether the limitations on participation are reasonably related to the efficient development and exploitation of the pooled technologies, and will assess their net effect in the relevant market`
      OTHERWISE JUST `exclusion is unlikely to have anticompetitive effects`

DECLARE `What section 5.5 says of a pool's terms on future technology` IS ONE OF
    `the arrangement is not likely to cause competitive problems: it does not include a large fraction of the potential research and development in a research and development market`

-- 5.5 [pdf page 33]: a pool requiring members to grant each other licenses for current and
-- future technology at minimal cost "is likely to cause competitive problems only when
-- the arrangement includes a large fraction of the potential research and development in
-- a research and development market". "Large fraction" has no figure in the text; it is
-- the caller's finding. When it is a large fraction the "only when" states nothing
-- (NOTHING).
@ref 5.5 [pdf page 33]
GIVEN p IS A `Pool terms on future technology (5.5)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.5 says of a pool's terms on future technology`
`5.5: a pool's grant of current and future technology at minimal cost` MEANS
    IF     p's `the pooling arrangement requires members to grant licenses to each other for current and future technology at minimal cost`
       AND (NOT p's `the arrangement includes a large fraction of the potential research and development in a research and development market`)
    THEN JUST `the arrangement is not likely to cause competitive problems: it does not include a large fraction of the potential research and development in a research and development market`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `5.6 Grantbacks`

DECLARE `What section 5.6 says of a grantback` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will evaluate the grantback provision under the rule of reason`

-- 5.6 [pdf page 35]: a grantback is "an arrangement under which a licensee agrees to extend
-- to the licensor ... the right to use the licensee's improvements to the licensed
-- technology"; "The Agencies will evaluate a grantback provision under the rule of
-- reason".
@ref 5.6 [pdf page 35]
GIVEN g IS A `Grantback facts (5.6)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.6 says of a grantback`
`5.6: a grantback` MEANS
    IF g's `the licensee agrees to extend to the licensor the right to use the licensee's improvements to the licensed technology`
    THEN JUST `the agencies will evaluate the grantback provision under the rule of reason`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Matter the agencies take into account for a grantback (5.6)` IS ONE OF
    `the exclusivity of the grantback`
    `the licensor's market power in a relevant technology or research and development market`
    `a likely significant reduction in licensees' incentives to invest in improving the licensed technology`
    `promotion of the dissemination of licensees' improvements`
    `greater incentives for licensors to disseminate the licensed technology`
    `other increases in competition and output in a relevant technology or research and development market`
    `greater incentives for licensors to innovate in the first place, from grantback provisions in the relevant markets generally`

-- 5.6 [pdf pages 35-36]: a non-exclusive grantback "is less likely to harm competition"
-- than an exclusive one; "An important factor ... will be whether the licensor has market
-- power"; "If the Agencies determine that a particular grantback provision is likely to
-- reduce significantly licensees' incentives to invest in improving the licensed
-- technology, the Agencies will consider the extent to which the grantback provision has
-- offsetting procompetitive effects, such as (1) ..., (2) ..., or (3) .... In addition,
-- the Agencies will consider the extent to which grantback provisions in the relevant
-- markets generally increase licensors' incentives to innovate". The offsetting effects
-- are reported only when that determination is made, as the text conditions them on it
-- (the "In addition" sentence is read as part of the same consideration; fork F12). A
-- report only; no weighing.
@ref 5.6 [pdf pages 35-36]
GIVEN g IS A `Grantback facts (5.6)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Matter the agencies take into account for a grantback (5.6)`
`5.6: the matters present` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF g's `the grantback is exclusive` THEN JUST `the exclusivity of the grantback` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF g's `the licensor has market power in a relevant technology or research and development market` THEN JUST `the licensor's market power in a relevant technology or research and development market` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF reduces THEN JUST `a likely significant reduction in licensees' incentives to invest in improving the licensed technology` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF reduces AND g's `it promotes dissemination of licensees' improvements to the licensed technology` THEN JUST `promotion of the dissemination of licensees' improvements` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF reduces AND g's `it increases the licensors' incentives to disseminate the licensed technology` THEN JUST `greater incentives for licensors to disseminate the licensed technology` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF reduces AND g's `it otherwise increases competition and output in a relevant technology or research and development market` THEN JUST `other increases in competition and output in a relevant technology or research and development market` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF reduces AND g's `grantback provisions in the relevant markets generally increase licensors' incentives to innovate in the first place` THEN JUST `greater incentives for licensors to innovate in the first place, from grantback provisions in the relevant markets generally` ELSE NOTHING))
  WHERE
    reduces MEANS g's `the grantback provision is likely to reduce significantly licensees' incentives to invest in improving the licensed technology`

§§ `5.7 Acquisition of intellectual property rights`

DECLARE `What section 5.7 says of a transfer` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will apply a merger analysis: an outright sale of all rights to the intellectual property`
    `the agencies will apply a merger analysis: an exclusive license`
    `the agencies may also apply a merger analysis: a license that in substance transfers the rights`
    `section 5.7 calls for no merger analysis`

-- 5.7 [pdf page 36]: "The Agencies will apply a merger analysis to an outright sale by an
-- intellectual property owner of all of its rights to that intellectual property and to
-- a transaction in which a person obtains through grant, sale, or other transfer an
-- exclusive license for intellectual property (i.e., a license that precludes all other
-- persons, including the licensor, from using the licensed intellectual property)."
-- n.85: they "may also apply a merger analysis" to a license that in substance transfers
-- the rights and may substantially lessen competition. Such transactions may be assessed
-- under section 7 of the Clayton Act, sections 1 and 2 of the Sherman Act and section 5
-- of the Federal Trade Commission Act. Exclusivity is in fact (Example 10; fork F8).
@ref 5.7, n.85 [pdf page 36]
GIVEN x IS A `Transfer of intellectual property rights (5.7)`
GIVETH A `What section 5.7 says of a transfer`
`5.7: merger analysis of the transfer` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF x's `the transfer is an outright sale by the owner of all of its rights to the intellectual property`
        THEN `the agencies will apply a merger analysis: an outright sale of all rights to the intellectual property`
      IF x's `the transferee obtains a license that in fact precludes all other persons, including the licensor, from using the licensed intellectual property`
        THEN `the agencies will apply a merger analysis: an exclusive license`
      IF x's `the license in substance transfers intellectual property rights, and its effect may be to substantially lessen competition in a relevant market`
        THEN `the agencies may also apply a merger analysis: a license that in substance transfers the rights`
      OTHERWISE `section 5.7 calls for no merger analysis`

§§ `6 Invalid or unenforceable intellectual property rights`

DECLARE `What section 6 says of the enforcement` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may challenge the enforcement of invalid intellectual property rights as an antitrust violation`
    `it may violate section 2 of the Sherman Act or section 5 of the Federal Trade Commission Act`
    `inequitable conduct short of knowing and willful fraud will not be the basis of a section 2 claim`
    `under some circumstances it may violate section 5 of the Federal Trade Commission Act`
    `sham litigation may constitute an element of a violation of the Sherman Act`

-- 6 [pdf pages 37-38]: "The Agencies may challenge the enforcement of invalid intellectual
-- property rights as antitrust violations. Enforcement or attempted enforcement of a
-- patent obtained by fraud on the Patent and Trademark Office may violate section 2 ... or
-- section 5 ..., if all the elements otherwise necessary to establish a charge are proved.
-- Inequitable conduct ... will not be the basis of a section 2 claim unless the conduct
-- also involves knowing and willful fraud and the other elements of a section 2 claim are
-- present. Actual or attempted enforcement of patents obtained by inequitable conduct that
-- falls short of fraud under some circumstances may violate section 5 ... In addition, sham
-- litigation to enforce intellectual property rights may also constitute an element of a
-- violation of the Sherman Act." Every statement that applies, in the text's order; none
-- when the conduct is not enforcement.
@ref 6 [pdf pages 37-38]
GIVEN x IS A `Enforcement of intellectual property rights (6)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What section 6 says of the enforcement`
`6: what section 6 says of the enforcement` MEANS
    IF NOT x's `the conduct is actual or attempted enforcement of the rights`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE catMaybes (LIST (IF x's `the rights enforced are invalid` THEN JUST `the agencies may challenge the enforcement of invalid intellectual property rights as an antitrust violation` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF fraud AND elements THEN JUST `it may violate section 2 of the Sherman Act or section 5 of the Federal Trade Commission Act` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF inequitable AND (NOT fraud) THEN JUST `inequitable conduct short of knowing and willful fraud will not be the basis of a section 2 claim` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF inequitable AND (NOT fraud) THEN JUST `under some circumstances it may violate section 5 of the Federal Trade Commission Act` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF x's `the enforcement is by sham litigation` THEN JUST `sham litigation may constitute an element of a violation of the Sherman Act` ELSE NOTHING))
  WHERE
    fraud MEANS x's `the patent was obtained by knowing and willful fraud on the Patent and Trademark Office`
    inequitable MEANS x's `the patent was obtained by inequitable conduct before the Patent and Trademark Office`
    elements MEANS x's `all the other elements necessary to establish the charge are proved`
